  "id": "Compound::DB00425 - Gene::2566",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Zolpidem and Gabrg2, primarily through its interaction with Gaba A receptors. Zolpidem, a central nervous system depressant, is included in the category of Gaba A agonists, which includes Zaleplon. Both Zolpidem and Zaleplon bind to Gabrg2, a subunit of the Gaba A receptor, thereby influencing its activity. This binding is supported by multiple paths, with varying scores indicating a consistent but not necessarily absolute relationship. The lowest common ancestors of Zolpidem and Zaleplon point to organic heterobicyclic compounds and sedative/hypnotic properties, reinforcing the role of these compounds in modulating neural activity. The evidence suggests that Zolpidem may act through Gabrg2 to exert its sedative effects, though the exact mechanism and the strength of this interaction require further investigation.",
